ONE IF-THEN BLOCK GOVERNS A SHORT AND A LONG INSTRUCTION.

A bottom-up English Major C example of the native ARMv7-A T32 ITSTATE
mechanism. The example begins with ACTUAL diagnostic object-file bytes
and does not pretend to be a compiled English Major C program.
It deliberately keeps the machine instructions in their actual
native order without inventing a higher-level source history.

The object was produced by the foreign diagnostic compiler clang
17.0.0, target armv7a-none-eabi in Thumb state, from:

    .syntax unified
    .arch armv7-a
    .thumb
    .text
    .global it_example
    .thumb_func
    it_example:
        cmp r0, #0
        ite eq
        moveq r1, r0
        movwne r2, #0x1234
        nop

This is a diagnostic assembler test, not a maintained ICK or Android
NDK build. The foreign decoder was LLVM objdump 17.0.0,
--triple=thumbv7-none-eabi.

The diagnostic source SHA256 is:
    30034378db9ad959282ba2c29a0c10ccd70884d2ecb1058df15ee532143dffc4
The diagnostic ELF32 ARM object SHA256 is:
    c2398cb4d78a63d12d0aff64ceb54351877b21f7a11e08887c3ded518043899c

THE ACTUAL DECODED MACHINE INSTRUCTIONS.

Offset zero:
    0x2800, a TWO-byte compare of R0 with the immediate zero.

Offset two:
    0xBF0C, a TWO-byte IT instruction with condition EQ and
    mask hexadecimal C. The diagnostic renders ITE EQ.

Offset four:
    0x4601, a TWO-byte MOV (register) from R0 to R1,
    governed by the first IT condition EQ.

Offset six:
    0xF241 followed by 0x2234, one FOUR-byte MOVW (immediate)
    instruction writing hexadecimal 0x1234 to R2,
    governed by the second IT condition NE.

Offset hexadecimal A:
    0xBF00, a TWO-byte NOP following the completed IT block.

The first halfword at offset six and the next halfword at offset
eight belong to the SAME native instruction. The second halfword
at offset eight is NOT an independent instruction whose effects
can be separately scheduled or skipped.

THE EXACT DECLARED SOURCE OPERATION IDENTITIES.

The compare maps to the existing signed/unsigned bit-pattern
comparison operation 14 (CMP), which sets N, Z, C, and V using
thirty-two-bit subtraction but does not keep an arithmetic result.

The IT maps to the existing operation 46, whose actual state
effect is an ITSTATE transition for TWO following instruction slots.

The MOV register maps to operation 01, which copies the 32-bit
pattern of its source register while preserving the four
condition flags. Its selected native instruction has exactly
the required non-flag-setting behavior.

The MOVW maps to operation 03, which writes a sixteen-bit
immediate as the LOW sixteen bits of a destination and sets
the HIGH sixteen bits to zero, preserving condition flags.

The NOP maps to the exact native BF00 form of operation 52.

No instruction outside the closed current 72-operation vocabulary
is needed to explain the normal-completion effects of this
short specimen. However, this source-level argument does NOT
constitute an independently accepted implementation of ITSTATE
or proof that every other permitted instruction is correct.

PART ONE. WHAT BF0C MEANS AS AN EXECUTION CONTRACT.

At offset two the halfword BF0C has:
  firstcond = binary 0000, the EQ condition;
  mask = binary 1100, whose trailing-zero count is two;
  number of following instruction slots = 4-2 = TWO.

Its first following instruction uses EQ, the condition Z=1.
Its second following instruction uses the inverse NE, Z=0.

Both following instructions are distinct instruction SLOTS,
but the first takes two bytes and the second takes FOUR.
The IT block therefore spans six bytes of following machine
instructions, from offsets 0x0004 through 0x0009 inclusive.
After the second conditional instruction has been passed,
ITSTATE is no longer active for the subsequent NOP.

The IT instruction itself changes neither R0 nor R1 nor R2.
It does not update N, Z, C or V. It establishes the conditional
state which will govern the next two instructions.
It is NOT a two-way direct branch and does not itself
replace the execution index with a distant branch target.

PART TWO. FIRST ACTUAL-BYTE TRACE, INPUT R0 EQUAL TO ZERO.

State is declared at offset 0x0000:
    R0=0;
    initial R1 and R2 are unknown;
    initial N/Z/C/V are unknown;
    executable mapping and T32 state assumed valid;
    no exception or asynchronous interruption occurs.

At offset 0x0000, CMP R0,#0 computes the bit pattern of 0-0,
which is zero. N becomes zero, Z becomes one, C becomes one
(no unsigned borrow), and V becomes zero (no signed overflow).
R0 remains zero. The next execution index is offset 0x0002.

At offset 0x0002, BF0C establishes two conditional slots.
The EQ condition for slot one will be true, and its inverse
NE for slot two will be false if flags remain unchanged.
The instruction itself leaves those flags unchanged.
The next execution index is offset 0x0004.

At offset 0x0004, MOV R1,R0 occupies TWO bytes and is
governed by the EQ condition. Z is one, so the permitted
register copy occurs: R1 becomes 0. R0 is preserved and
the condition flags are preserved. ITSTATE advances to
the second slot. The next index is offset 0x0006.

At offset 0x0006, the first halfword F241 denotes the
beginning of a FOUR-byte MOVW instruction, whose second
halfword 2234 begins at offset 0x0008. The current IT slot
uses NE. Since Z is one, the NE condition is false.
The destination-register effect is suppressed: R2 keeps its
prior UNKNOWN value. The full four-byte instruction
still occupies addresses 0x0006 through 0x0009.
After its conditional non-execution and the appropriate
ITSTATE update, the next execution index is offset 0x000A.
Do NOT invent an independent instruction at 0x0008.

At offset 0x000A, BF00 is the ordinary TWO-byte NOP
outside the completed IT block. It changes no general
register or condition flag. Ordinary execution makes
the next index offset 0x000C, beyond the supplied fixture.

The visited instruction-boundary offsets are:
    0x0000, 0x0002, 0x0004, 0x0006, 0x000A.
At the first unavailable instruction boundary, 0x000C,
the known values are:
    R0=0; R1=0; R2 is unknown;
    N=0; Z=1; C=1; V=0.

No return, operating system call, branch or other
instruction is invented after offset 0x000C.

PART THREE. SECOND TRACE, SAME MACHINE BYTES, INPUT R0 EQUAL TO SEVEN.

Begin again at offset 0x0000 with:
    R0=7;
    R1 and R2 unknown;
    initial flags unknown.

At offset zero, CMP R0,#0 computes 7-0 and sets:
    N=0; Z=0; C=1; V=0.
R0 remains seven; next index 0x0002.

At 0x0002, BF0C establishes the exact same two-slot
IT block as in the first trace. The instruction bytes
did not change between runs.

At 0x0004, MOV R1,R0 is conditional on EQ, which is now
FALSE because Z=0. R1 remains UNKNOWN. The processor's
architectural sequential execution still advances
across the TWO-byte slot to 0x0006, and ITSTATE
advances to the second condition.

At 0x0006, the FOUR-byte MOVW R2,#0x1234 is governed
by NE, which is now TRUE. The native operation writes
the exact 32-bit value 0x00001234 to R2.
The flags N=0,Z=0,C=1,V=0 remain unchanged.
The next architectural instruction boundary is 0x000A.

At 0x000A, BF00 executes NOP outside IT and
ordinary sequential execution reaches 0x000C.

The visited instruction-boundary offsets are the SAME:
    0x0000, 0x0002, 0x0004, 0x0006, 0x000A.
But the register effects are DIFFERENT:
    R0=7;
    R1 remains unknown;
    R2=0x00001234;
    N=0; Z=0; C=1; V=0.

A change in conditional execution can thus change
register effects without changing the ordered
instruction addresses visited. It is incorrect
to infer from identical execution-index traces
that identical register writes occurred.

PART FOUR. WHY THIS IS NOT A BRANCH-TO-SKIP CONSTRUCTION.

No conditional B opcode appears in this diagnostic.
The machine fetches the conditionally governed instruction
encodings in sequential order and uses architectural
ITSTATE to determine whether their normal data effects
occur. The first instruction is TWO bytes and the
second is FOUR, but the IT block has exactly TWO SLOTS.

An alternative sequence might use a CMP and two
explicit B conditional instructions to select
different straight-line blocks. Such a program
could produce the same final register values
for selected inputs, but its actual branch
targets, instruction fetches and possible
prediction effects would differ.

The source representation MUST retain whether
the native bytes contained IT or actual B.
An interpreter that materializes only the final
register values has not yet provided the complete
instruction-by-instruction English translation
that this language experiment demands.

PART FIVE. RETAINED ALTERNATIVES AND THEIR CONSEQUENCES.

Alternative A: represent IT as a first-class state transition
and every following instruction as a distinct operation.
This best matches the actual instruction bytes and preserves
instruction-size and flag-state evidence. It requires
ITSTATE in the abstract machine.

Alternative B: normalize an IT block to conditional branch
edges and then ordinary register writes. This may help
later higher-level intermediate representations, but a
machine-faithful first translation must keep the original
bytes and the normalization proof.

Alternative C: use a conditional move or select when a target
architecture supports an appropriately matched instruction.
Its availability and effects must be verified per ISA,
not assumed from the English word "conditional".

Alternative D: choose a source-level masking operation to
compute the same result without IT or B. Arithmetic/bitwise
work and register/flag effects differ and require independent
equivalence testing.

Alternative E: compile the same English source on a different
ARM architectural target, possibly one that deprecates
multi-slot IT or restricts allowed instruction forms. A
different target feature profile and legality proof would
be required.

Alternative F: store the original IT block's bits as plain
DATA and let a separate interpreter inspect them. The CPU
would execute the interpreter's instructions, not directly
the byte pattern being interpreted. That representation
is distinct from the native sequence observed above.

No alternative is selected as a universally faster
implementation merely by counting branches or instructions.

PART SIX. NEGATIVE CASES AN ACCEPTED LANGUAGE MUST REJECT.

1. Treat BF0C as a branch to the second data operation.
2. Count BF0C as FOUR following instructions because its mask
   has four bit positions.
3. Claim that four instruction bytes always represent two IT slots.
4. Execute the second halfword 2234 as its own instruction.
5. Fail to advance from offset 0x0006 to 0x000A after the
   four-byte conditional MOVW is skipped.
6. Change N/Z/C/V during MOV R1,R0 or MOVW R2,#0x1234
   when their specified native forms preserve flags.
7. Perform MOVW's R2 write in the R0=0 trace.
8. Perform MOV's R1 write in the R0=7 trace.
9. Treat R2's unknown initial value as zero when MOVW is skipped.
10. Claim that an identical sequence of instruction-boundary
    addresses implies identical register changes.
11. Place an explicitly prohibited T1 conditional B inside
    the same IT block without changing the language/ISA contract.
12. Claim a physical ARM run, real branch-prediction behavior
    or independently accepted English Major C compiler from
    the existence of this diagnostic object and source text.

This diagnostic confirms byte encoding and the stated
source-level architectural reasoning only. It is not
a completed executable English Major C implementation.

END OF TWO-WIDTH ITSTATE TRACE.
